# G068 — Bulk Thumbnail Creator

*v2. Closes Threading. See `bulk-thumbnail.md` for prose: result as
shared aggregated index, content-addressing as parallel-safe dedup,
reservation pattern decoupling lock-hold from work-time, CPU-bound
ergonomics, partitioned locks.*

---

## Choreographic Case: Parallel Vault Thumbnail Generation

[generate-vault-thumbnails @worker-count
    IN ORDER TO
        walk every image in the vault, produce a deduplicated thumbnail
        index keyed by content hash, and run the expensive thumbnailer
        at most once per unique content across all workers

    @images  <- [vault[images]]
    @creator <- [thumbnails[new {worker-count: @worker-count}]]

    for @img in @images
        [thumbnails[enqueue
                     {creator: @creator,
                      req: {source-id: @img:path,
                             content: [fs[read {path: @img:path}]]}}]]

    @index <- [thumbnails[run-with
                            {creator: @creator,
                             hasher:       [content[sha256]],
                             thumbnailer:  [image[resize-to
                                                    {dims: [200, 200]}]]}]]

    [log[write
          {level: "info",
           message: @index:generated + " generated, " +
                     @index:deduplicated + " deduped"}]]
]

---

## Verb Surface

[thumbnails
    new                  {worker-count}
    enqueue              {creator, req}
    run-with             {creator, hasher, thumbnailer}
                                                  ;; -> Index
    extract              {index, content-hash}     ;; -> thumbnail | none
    source-to-hash       {index, source-id}        ;; -> hash
]

---

## Distinctive Innate idiom: content-addressing `where` hash IS the key

[thumbnails[run-with {creator: @c, hasher: @h, thumbnailer: @th}]
    IN ORDER TO
        compute the thumbnail exactly once per unique content
        even when many workers race on the same bytes concurrently

    for each job popped from @c:queue
        @hash <- [@h {content: @job:content}]
        acquire @c:index-lock
            where @hash already in @c:index
                -> record (@job:source-id → @hash) in source-map
                   (dedup — skip expensive work)
            otherwise
                -> insert placeholder under @hash in @c:index
                -> record (@job:source-id → @hash) in source-map
                -> mark "I own the generation for this hash"
        release @c:index-lock

        where "I own generation"
            @thumb <- [@th {content: @job:content}]   ;; EXPENSIVE, no lock held
            acquire @c:index-lock
                install @thumb at @hash (replace placeholder)
            release
]

Two workers with identical content compute the same hash; one claims
the placeholder, the other sees a "present" entry and records dedup.
Same discipline Nix caches, Docker layers, Git objects, IPFS use.

## Distinctive Innate idiom: reservation decouples lock-hold `where` work is expensive

The critical sections are TINY:
1. Check + insert placeholder + record mapping   (fast)
2. Install real thumbnail                         (fast)

The expensive thumbnailer runs OUTSIDE any lock. Concurrent workers
that hit the placeholder treat it as dedup, semantically correct
because someone else IS generating this thumbnail right now.

Same pattern Go's sync.Once, Rust's OnceLock, and memoise-under-
concurrency libraries all use — G068 shows the primitive explicitly.

## Distinctive Innate idiom: partitioned locks `where` concerns are orthogonal

[thumbnails[run-with]
    uses TWO locks:
        queue-lock   — protects the job queue (pop)
        index-lock   — protects the shared thumbnail index (claim/install)

    operations on the queue do not affect the index.
    A worker waiting on queue-lock does not block another worker
    installing into the index.
]

Contention on each lock is independent. Production scales by
sharding index-lock by hash prefix; the demo uses one global lock.

---

## Demo

[demo
    @c <- [thumbnails[new {worker-count: 4}]]
    [thumbnails[enqueue
                 {creator: @c,
                  req: {source-id: "a.png", content: [1, 2, 3]}}]]
    [thumbnails[enqueue
                 {creator: @c,
                  req: {source-id: "b.png", content: [1, 2, 3]}}]]   ;; dup
    [thumbnails[enqueue
                 {creator: @c,
                  req: {source-id: "c.png", content: [4, 5, 6]}}]]

    @index <- [thumbnails[run-with
                            {creator: @c,
                             hasher: @sha, thumbnailer: @thumbnailer}]]

    -> @index:generated                        ;; -> 2 (not 3)
    -> @index:deduplicated                     ;; -> 1
    -> [thumbnails[extract
                     {index: @index,
                      content-hash:
                        [thumbnails[source-to-hash
                                      {index: @index,
                                       source-id: "a.png"}]]}]]
       is equal to
       [thumbnails[extract
                     {index: @index,
                      content-hash:
                        [thumbnails[source-to-hash
                                      {index: @index,
                                       source-id: "b.png"}]]}]]
    ;; -> true (a.png and b.png share one thumbnail)
]
